{"id":97534809347760,"type":"album","name":"Rickie Lee Jones","artist":"Rickie Lee Jones","year":1979,"weight":16,"info":{"cover":"https://ia801902.us.archive.org/13/items/mbid-19bd2ed9-1fba-4331-8a92-ae077b9f682d/mbid-19bd2ed9-1fba-4331-8a92-ae077b9f682d-3731902360.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":11,"country":"XE","scaruffi":8.0,"yt":"Z2vShCwS54Y","links":{"spotify":"https://open.spotify.com/album/0ccoup8J5qvnANkKtERuDd","youtube":"https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_mVWI4oHkHsEZgMyPJbctHIRnkXv-Na7o0","rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/rickie-lee-jones/rickie-lee-jones"},"desc":"Millions were introduced to Rickie Lee Jones via her hit single “Chuck E.’s In Love,” a slinky hipster valentine, but those who dug into her 1979 self-titled debut album discovered that Ms. Jones offered more than flirtatious beatnik jive. Her serious intentions as a vocalist and composer are evident throughout these tracks, produced with care by Lenny Waronker and Russ Titelman. Jones’ persona — by turns coy, street-smart and vulnerable — is delineated in lyrics filled with evocative slang and visionary poetry. She’s an especially deft story-teller, catching the pathos of a down-and-out mother in “Night Train” and the charm ."},"artist_ref":{"id":96106856615469,"name":"Rickie Lee Jones","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rickie_Lee_Jones_at_3_Rivers.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":185670346256268,"type":"genre","name":"rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":3782,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":210192360016800,"type":"genre","name":"Alternative Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":2664,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":58867391113657,"type":"genre","name":"Jazz Pop","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":218,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":129865811082200,"type":"genre","name":"funk / soul","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":45,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"}],"lists":[{"id":78999271193490,"name":"RYM Top — Po